{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790717301,"nonce":"HAbdagm1oBNW09EkUFBRl5NJRVc=","title":"Dynamic call bridge drops arguments that require stack slots","message":"Inspection of std/src/fun.rs found that ash_static_call marshals only register arguments when hlp_call_method invokes a function through runtime reflection. On AArch64 it loops over nargs.min(8); on x86-64 System V it ignores integer arguments after the sixth and FP arguments after the eighth; on Win64 it loops over nargs.min(4). The code does not build stack argument slots, so higher-arity dynamic calls may silently receive missing or wrong arguments. This is inferred from the bridge implementation and still needs a small executable reproducer on each supported ABI.\n\nAdd a typed Reflect.callMethod test with arguments beyond the register slots, including mixed integer/f32/f64 cases. Marshal stack arguments with the target ABI's alignment and shadow-space rules, or route through a generated signature trampoline. Verify return values on AArch64, x86-64 System V, and Win64. Keep the WebAssembly per-signature trampoline path separate.","files":null}]}